WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
You know who's next, make some noise, start it now!

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.640
For Markiplier!

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:10.420
Hello!

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.360
Hi!

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.100
Welcome, welcome, welcome.
Why is there another microphone here?

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.140
I probably just screwed up the whole 
show by doing that.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.360
But, Hello. Hi, How's it going?

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.040
My name is Markiplier, and I'm here to
share something with you.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.960
When they asked me to come out on this stage,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.580
I knew that i was going to be among
Other guests that were much more talented

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.860
and able to actually sing and dance
and do things like that...

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.120
You know, it's true.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.120
But, the one thing they asked me to do was share something with you that I love,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.940
And as some of you may know, as
some of you may not know.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.820
The one thing that i really truly love is, Space!

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.860
Lot of space lovers out here...
- I LOVE space.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.840
And when they ask me to share this thing...

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.500
and no, I'm not going to sing the song, sorry!

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.660
Ooh... Boo... ohhh...

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:23.300
No, no, no, I wanted to tell you
guys the story of why I love space.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.840
And that really got me thinking about
you know, where that came from

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.540
where I fostered this deep connection
to what amounts to....

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.240
...a big nothing up there.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:34.880
Umm...

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.260
But it really does go deep with me because,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.680
if I were to tell the story, I would
have to go back to

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.580
My childhood, when I was about 12.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.300
My parents had just gotten divorced, and we didn't

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.080
have a lot of money for video games

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.280
But my dad bought me this game
called Homeworld,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.620
and I don't know if you guys know about this game

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.780
It's alright if you don't, but it's basically
a game about space.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:06.740
And I loved it, i loved it number one,
because my dad spent what little money he had,

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.300
on this game, for me to enjoy.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.820
And number two...

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.040
It was really a good game!

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.580
So I played the crap out of it the music
was great the art was great.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.920
The vastness of it was wonderful

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.260
But, twelve year old me really didn't 
appreciate it for what it was at the time

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.700
So I played it through a couple times,
and then shelved it for later

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.440
The next time that I thought about it

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.580
Was about a week after my dad passed away,

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.000
I was

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:36.800
I was in

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.920
Still in shock. I hadn't let myself grieve about it

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:43.850
And I found myself

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:50.259
I was working a deck building job as some of you may know and I went up to my boss because that particular day

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:56.350
I was feeling really off and as I should and I told him I needed to take a break

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.589
He didn't deserve like I didn't deserve to be paid if I wasn't going to work well

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:05.049
So I left and I went to my mom's house, and I I laid down in her driveway

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:08.019
and I just stared up and I look at the stars

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:16.899
And I stayed there for hours and hours, and I was just comforted by the emptiness of it. You know

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.480
I wasn't looking for any answers. I wasn't looking for anything to shout out at me. I was just looking up

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:29.469
Because I needed the silence I needed the solace I needed the solitude

00:03:30.079 --> 00:03:32.079
And then I had a thought

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:38.139
something I hadn't thought about since I was a child I thought about that game

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:42.160
And I realized that I really wanted to play it again

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.259
And I wasn't sure why I wanted to play it again. I just had to play it again. So I went over to

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:54.250
My step mom and dad's house and in their basement my dad was a creature of habit

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:58.449
He stored everything meticulously that he ever had

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:02.109
Every book that he read every book that he published every

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:08.828
Every piece of cookware that was passed down to our family old grills that didn't work anymore

00:04:09.139 --> 00:04:13.659
You know but in the back in the very back among all these bookshelves

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:16.590
was a game shelf

00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:23.909
within meticulous order every single game that he had bought me over the years

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.840
And

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:32.730
Walking in I didn't realize it, and it wasn't until I pulled that game from the shelf and I looked back and I saw

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:36.510
This catalog of my dad's life

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.520
Then I realized

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.330
just how much I missed him

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:45.510
And so I broke down and I was crying and

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.310
crying, but then I

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:49.080
Open the game

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.489
And this is one game boxes used to be big so it's big big instruction manual pop the CD

00:04:53.490 --> 00:05:01.140
And install it back in days when you had to install games of CDs, and then I played it, and I loved it

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:08.249
I loved everything about it. And I was grown up enough to appreciate how big it was out there

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:15.209
And then when I came back from playing the game. I was like 16 hours straight. Just stuck in my my stepmoms basement

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.850
Just playing over and over again crying intermittently

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:22.980
And then just though I and then I went back outside it was dark and I looked up

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.640
And it didn't seem empty anymore

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.359
It was everything

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:38.849
I understood, how vast it really was and I understood how big it was and how small I am

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.730
And that's not a short joke. It's not a short joke

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:45.788
But that's when I realized that

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:54.519
I had to let my dad go and I gained an appreciation for something that's so much bigger than myself

00:05:56.419 --> 00:05:59.409
So anyway, that's what I wanted to share with you

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:04.090
Thank you everybody

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.090
I'll see you in the next one

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:08.500
Bye bye

